The peaked field does separate policies (peaks-6, P_need=1: sighted and intact live 6, stay 6, oracle 7, random 6), so the medium is no longer silent. But the question it asks is not the memory question. (1) More information is WORSE: the oracle, which knows the globally richest cell, dies at 7 while the local rule lives 6, because it leaves a cell that pays the upkeep and spends the crossing on poor cells. (2) Memory adds nothing: `intact` and `sighted` produce the same lifetime and a difference that is only the tie-break, because a memory slot does not save a single read - a look at the two neighbours costs the same whether or not something is remembered. (3) The price does bite (uniform, P_need=2, sighted: 24 at read_cost=0 against 17 at 0.05, while at read_budget=1, where no neighbour is read, it is 24 against 24), so the mechanism is real - it just presses on every reader equally. On `uniform` 155's equality is between oracle and erased (20000 against 20000); the brief's oracle-vs-random claim does not reproduce and never did: the stay-capable walker dies at 60. So the answer to 'does the priced, peaked field pose the memory question' is NO, and `not applicable` is wrong for it: an effect exists, it is just not about memory. The verdict is `failed`.
